  <info>
    <meta name="maintainer" content="amrita.sakthivel@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
    <title>Troubleshooting software RAID devices</title>
    <abstract>
<para>
  Troubleshooting RAID is critical because RAID provides redundancy, not a true backup—meaning an undetected drive failure leaves your system just one disk away from total data loss. Timely diagnosis ensures you can replace failing drives and rebuild degraded arrays before a secondary failure leads to catastrophic downtime.
</para>
    </abstract>
  </info>
  <section xml:id="failed-disk">
<title>Array is degraded or running with a failed disk</title>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="bold">Symptom:</emphasis> The command <command>cat /proc/mdstat</command> shows missing disk flags like [U_] instead of [UU], or system alerts report an active disk failure.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="bold">Solution:</emphasis> Fail, remove, replace, and trigger array rebuild.</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Mark drive failed (if kernel hasn't already):</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; mdadm /dev/md0 --fail /dev/sdb1</screen>
</listitem>
<listitem><para>Remove failed partition:</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;  mdadm /dev/md0 --remove /dev/sdb1 </screen>
  </listitem>
  <listitem><para>Clone partition layout to new disk:</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; sfdisk -d /dev/sda | sfdisk /dev/sdb</screen>
    </listitem>
    <listitem><para>Add new disk partition:</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;  mdadm /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdb1</screen>
      </listitem>
      <listitem><para>Monitor resync progress:</para>
 <screen>&prompt.sudo; watch -n 1 cat /proc/mdstat</screen>
        </listitem>
</orderedlist>
</listitem>
</itemizedlist>
  </section>
   <section xml:id="array-assemble">
        <title> Array refuses to assemble after a reboot or power outage</title>
        <itemizedlist>
          <listitem><para><emphasis role="bold">Symptom:</emphasis>  System drops to emergency shell, or <filename>/dev/md0</filename> does not exist because drive ordering shifted (e.g., /dev/sdb became /dev/sdc).
          </para></listitem>
          <listitem><para><emphasis role="bold">Solution:</emphasis> Auto-scan and force assembly using drive metadata superblocks.</para>
          <orderedlist>
          <listitem><para>Discover and assemble inactive arrays:</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; mdadm --assemble --scan</screen>
          </listitem>
          <listitem><para>If an array dropped out-of-sync drives, force start it: </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; mdadm --assemble --force /dev/md0 /dev/sda1 /dev/sdb1</screen>
            </listitem>
            <listitem><para>Update <filename>/etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf</filename> with persistent UUIDs:</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; mdadm --detail --scan &gt;&gt; /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf</screen>
              </listitem>
              <listitem><para>Update initramfs to fix boot failure:</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; dracut -f</screen>
                </listitem>
          </orderedlist>
          </listitem>
          </itemizedlist>
          </section>
          <section xml:id="data-corrupion">
            <title> Silent data corruption or parity inconsistency</title>
            <itemizedlist>
              <listitem><para><emphasis role="bold">Symptom:</emphasis> File system errors occur despite the array reporting all member drives as healthy ([UU]).
              </para></listitem>
              <listitem><para><emphasis role="bold">Solution:</emphasis> Run array scrubbing and check status logs.</para>
              <orderedlist>
              <listitem><para>Trigger background data check:</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; echo check &gt; /sys/block/md0/md/sync_action
    </screen>
              </listitem>
              <listitem><para>Check mismatch count (0 means healthy): </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; cat /sys/block/md0/md/mismatch_cnt </screen>
                </listitem>
                <listitem><para>Inspect kernel logs for bad sectors:</para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo; dmesg | grep -E "md|sd|nvme|I/O error"</screen>
                  </listitem>
                  <listitem><para>Repair inconsistencies if non-zero:</para>
    <screen>&prompt.sudo; echo repair &gt; /sys/block/md0/md/sync_action
    </screen>
                    </listitem>
              </orderedlist>
              </listitem>
              </itemizedlist>
              </section>
    </topic>